1 CONFIGURATION
   govax keeps configuration settings in a profile in the .govax
   directory of your home directory.  They are read each time govax
   starts.  The settings are set and shown with the govax command's
   CONFIG verb, from the host shell, not from the VAX> prompt:

       govax config set KEY=VALUE      Set a setting.  A KEY alone,
                                       with no =VALUE, sets it to true.
       govax config show               Show every setting in the profile.
       govax config show KEY           Show one setting's value.
       govax config delete KEY         Remove a setting, so its default
                                       applies again.
       govax config list               List the profiles.

   The global option --profile NAME (before the verb) chooses a profile
   other than the default one, for example:

       govax --profile test config set vax.quantum=50
       govax --profile test

   For example, to have govax create (the first time) and mount a work
   volume each time it starts, and make it the default:

       govax config set vax.default.volume.file="~/vax/work.dsk"

   Keys are case-sensitive and written in lowercase.  govax warns at
   exit about any key in the profile it doesn't recognize, which usually
   means a misspelling.

   For the list of keys and what each one does, type:

       HELP CONFIG KEYS
2 KEYS
   These are the configuration settings govax reads (see HELP CONFIG
   for how to set them).

   The system volume

       govax mounts a system volume, labeled GOVAXSYS, every time it
       starts, before anything else: an ODS-2 container holding govax's
       own files (its startup procedure, grammars, help, libraries, and
       FTNRTL.EXE, with the sources and procedures that rebuild them).
       The first time, govax makes it from the files built into it:

           %MOUNT-I-CREATED, container /home/me/.govax/govax-system.dsk
               created as an RD54 volume labeled GOVAXSYS
           %MOUNT-I-MOUNTED, GOVAXSYS mounted on _DUA0:

       Only one govax session at a time can write it.  A second session
       mounts it read-only (%MOUNT-I-SYSREADONLY).  If it can't be used
       (it can't be made or mounted, or its label isn't GOVAXSYS), a
       %MOUNT-W-NOSYSVOL warning says why, and govax starts from its
       built-in files.  DISMOUNT won't dismount it.

       Each start runs the volume's SYS$MANAGER:STARTUP.COM, which
       defines SYS$SYSTEM, SYS$MANAGER, SYS$HELP, SYS$LIBRARY,
       SYS$SHARE, and SYS$SOURCES (govax itself defines SYS$SYSDEVICE,
       the volume's device, and SYS$SYSROOT, its [SYSTEM] directory as
       a root),
       and runs your SYS$MANAGER:SYSTARTUP_VMS.COM last.  The console's
       and the debugger's grammars are the volume's copies too, unless
       one is in the current directory or a --path directory; one that
       can't be read, or a grammar that doesn't parse or lacks one of
       govax's commands, is a %VAX-W-SYSGRAMMAR warning, and govax uses
       its own.  HELP reads the volume's help libraries in SYS$HELP.  A
       newer govax replaces each of its files you haven't changed with
       its own copy, as a new version; one you have changed is kept, and
       %MOUNT-I-SYSKEPT says so, once for each new copy.

       The options --system-file FILE and --system-device DEVICE take
       precedence over the settings below for one run, and
       --system-file none starts govax with no system volume at all.
       --system-initialize makes the volume again from govax's own
       files, keeping the old container beside it as FILE.old: the way
       back from a system volume that no longer works.

       vax.default.system.file       The container's host path, or none.
                                     A leading ~/ is your home
                                     directory.  Default
                                     ~/.govax/govax-system.dsk.

       vax.default.system.device     The device it is mounted on.  A
                                     device with no unit number (DUA) is
                                     that controller's first unit with
                                     nothing mounted on it.  Default
                                     DUA0.

       vax.default.system.type       The disk type (RD51, RD54, RA81,
                                     ...) that sizes a new container,
                                     and that the device is defined as.
                                     Default RD54 (311,200 blocks; the
                                     host file takes only the space its
                                     files use).

   The default volume

       When vax.default.volume.file is set, govax mounts that ODS-2
       container at startup and does a SET DEFAULT to it, so file
       commands (DIRECTORY, TYPE, MACRO, LINK, RUN, ...) work on the
       volume rather than on the host's files.  A container that doesn't
       exist is created and initialized first, with the default
       directory in it:

           %MOUNT-I-CREATED, container /home/me/vax/work.dsk created as
               an RD54 volume labeled WORK
           %MOUNT-I-MOUNTED, WORK mounted on _DUA1:

       A container that can only be read is mounted read-only, and
       %MOUNT-I-WRITELOCK says so.  If the volume can't be used (it
       can't be created, opened, or mounted, its label doesn't match,
       or the device is already mounted, by --mount or as the system
       volume), a
       %MOUNT-W-NODEFAULT warning says why and govax starts without it.
       With no container configured, file names go to the host as they
       always have.

       vax.default.volume.file       The container's host path.  A
                                     leading ~/ is your home directory.
                                     Unset: no default volume.

       vax.default.volume.label      The volume label a new container is
                                     given.  When set, an existing
                                     container's label must match it, or
                                     the volume isn't used.  Default WORK
                                     (and no check).

       vax.default.volume.device     The device the volume is mounted on.
                                     A device with no unit number (DUA)
                                     is that controller's first unit
                                     with nothing mounted on it.
                                     Default DUA: DUA1, beside the
                                     system volume on DUA0.

       vax.default.volume.type       The disk type (RX50, RD54, RA81, ...)
                                     that sizes a new container, and that
                                     the device is defined as.  Default
                                     RD54 (311,200 blocks).

       vax.default.volume.directory  The default directory, created in
                                     a new container's MFD.  If it isn't
                                     on the volume, the default is the
                                     MFD, [000000].  Default [WORK].

   Files and libraries

       vax.path                      Host directories to search for
                                     files a console command names, after
                                     the name as given, separated by
                                     commas (like the --path option).

       vax.library                   The host directory LINK and MACRO
                                     look in for the system object and
                                     macro libraries and shareable
                                     images, when SYS$LIBRARY and
                                     SYS$SHARE don't lead to them.

       vax.link.library              The older name for vax.library,
                                     read when vax.library isn't set.

   The console

       vax.startup.procedure         If specified, the name of a DCL
                                     command procedure run automatically
                                     during startup processing.  If the
                                     procedure name does not exist, or
                                     is empty, there is no error.

       vax.console.prompt            The console's prompt.  Default
                                     "VAX> ".  SET PROMPT changes it.

       vax.console.lines             The screen's height in lines for
                                     TYPE/PAGE, used when the terminal's
                                     own size can't be read (output not
                                     to a terminal).  Default 24.

       vax.disassemble.symbolic      true or false: whether DISASSEMBLE
                                     and SHOW CALLS show the VMS
                                     debugger's symbolic layout by
                                     default.  Default true.

       vax.editor.command            The host command EDIT runs to edit
                                     a file, %file standing for the file;
                                     without one, the file goes last.
                                     SET EDITOR/COMMAND changes it.
                                     Default "vi %file".

       vax.editor.scratch            The host directory EDIT works in.
                                     SET EDITOR/SCRATCH changes it.
                                     Default the host's temporary
                                     directory.

       vax.language                  The user's language, as
                                     LIB$GET_USERS_LANGUAGE returns it
                                     (FRENCH, GERMAN, ...) when the
                                     SYS$LANGUAGE logical name isn't
                                     defined.  When neither is set, the
                                     host's locale (LC_ALL, LC_MESSAGES,
                                     or LANG) gives it; failing that,
                                     the routine returns ENGLISH and
                                     LIB$_ENGLUSED.

       vax.runtime.random            When true, MTH$RANDOM's numbers come
                                     from the host's cryptographic
                                     generator, and can't be predicted or
                                     repeated; the seed is rewritten, but
                                     its old value plays no part.  When
                                     false, each follows from the seed by
                                     the VMS manual's formula, so a seed
                                     repeats its sequence.  Default false.

   The machine

       vax.hardware.clock            true or false: drive the interval
                                     clock and time-of-year clock from
                                     the host's real time rather than
                                     from instruction counts.  Default
                                     false.

       vax.quantum                   The number of instructions between
                                     clock ticks when vax.hardware.clock
                                     is false.  Default 20.

   Memory

       These are read by the startup procedure, STARTUP.COM (with
       F$CONFIG), for its INITIALIZE/VAX and INITIALIZE/MEMORY commands;
       each is a number of 512-byte pages, a DCL integer (decimal, or %X
       for hex).  One that isn't set keeps STARTUP.COM's own value.  A
       STARTUP.COM you have changed need not read them.

       vax.memory.size               Physical memory (INITIALIZE/VAX).
                                     Default 16384 (8MB).

       vax.memory.p0                 INITIALIZE/MEMORY's P0 region.
                                     Default 16384, or its paging
                                     default with paging on.

       vax.memory.p1                 INITIALIZE/MEMORY's P1 region.
                                     Default 8192, or its paging
                                     default with paging on.

       vax.memory.s0                 INITIALIZE/MEMORY's S0 region.
                                     Default 8192, or its paging
                                     default with paging on.

       vax.memory.ksp                The kernel stack.  Default 20.

       vax.memory.esp                The executive stack.  Default 8.

       vax.memory.ssp                The supervisor stack.  Default 8.

       vax.memory.isp                The interrupt stack.  Default 4.

       With paging off, a region given 0 shares, with the others given
       0, the physical memory the rest leave, and physical memory still
       left over is added to S0.  With paging on
       (vax.sysgen.paging.enable), STARTUP.COM gives INITIALIZE/MEMORY
       0 for each region not set here, and its paging defaults apply
       (see HELP INITIALIZE /MEMORY).

   Processes

       These are for running several processes at once.  With
       vax.process.scheduler on (the default), a program can create
       processes ($CREPRC, and LIB$SPAWN), and the CPU is shared among them
       VMS's way: by priority, a quantum at a time among equals, and
       giving the CPU to another while one waits.  Mailboxes, common
       event flags, global sections, the lock services ($ENQ, $ENQW,
       $DEQ), and shared files (see MOUNT) work between them, and a
       process waiting for a line from the terminal lets the others
       run.  SHOW SYSTEM lists them, and STOP deletes one.  With it
       off, only the console's own process runs, and $CREPRC and
       LIB$SPAWN return %SYSTEM-E-UNSUPPORTED.

       A process LIB$SPAWN creates, or $CREPRC creates to run
       LOGINOUT.EXE, runs a small command interpreter in place of DCL.
       It runs one command (LIB$SPAWN's command-string) or reads its
       commands from SYS$INPUT (the terminal, a mailbox, or a file),
       running each command's image in turn in the same process.  It
       knows RUN, MCR, foreign commands and other DCL symbols, symbol
       assignments, DELETE/SYMBOL, SHOW SYMBOL, SHOW LOGICAL, EXIT,
       and LOGOUT; anything else is %DCL-W-IVVERB.  LOGINOUT's,
       reading a file or a mailbox, echoes each command as it reads
       it, as DCL's verify does in a batch job, and ends with
       LOGOUT's accounting report.

       vax.process.scheduler         true or false: whether processes
                                     other than the console's own can be
                                     created and run.  Default true.

       vax.process.quantum           The number of instructions a
                                     process runs before another process
                                     of the same priority may have the
                                     CPU.  Default 20000.

       vax.process.preempt           Which access modes a process can
                                     lose the CPU in, when its quantum
                                     ends or a higher-priority process
                                     can run: all (as on VMS), user
                                     (user mode only), or none (only
                                     when the process waits).  Default
                                     all; govax warns at startup about
                                     any other value.

   System parameters

   The vax.sysgen.* keys are govax's SYSGEN parameters: each is named
   for the VMS SYSGEN parameter it stands for, in lowercase.  Counts
   are in 512-byte pages, as a decimal integer or %X followed by hex
   digits; govax warns at startup about a value that isn't a positive
   integer, and uses the default.  The values that start a process off
   are copied into each process when it's created.

       vax.sysgen.paging.enable      true or false: whether
                                     INITIALIZE/MEMORY turns VMS
                                     paging on (see HELP INITIALIZE
                                     /MEMORY /PAGING).  Default false.

       vax.sysgen.pql_dwsdefault     PQL_DWSDEFAULT: a new process's
                                     default working set size.  Default
                                     294 for a process $CREPRC creates;
                                     the console's process has the
                                     SYSTEM account's 512.

       vax.sysgen.pql_dwsquota       PQL_DWSQUOTA: a new process's
                                     working set quota, the pages it is
                                     guaranteed.  Default 588; the
                                     console's process has 1024.

       vax.sysgen.pql_dwsextent      PQL_DWSEXTENT: a new process's
                                     working set extent, the most its
                                     working set may grow to while free
                                     pages allow.  Default 16400.

       vax.sysgen.pql_dpgflquota     PQL_DPGFLQUOTA: a new job's paging
                                     file quota, the pages its processes
                                     may together have in the page file.
                                     Default 16400; the console's job
                                     has 40960.  With paging on, pages
                                     are charged as they are created,
                                     and $CRETVA, $EXPREG, and LIB$GET_VM
                                     fail past it.

       vax.sysgen.minwscnt           MINWSCNT: the smallest working set
                                     a process may have.  Default 20.

       vax.sysgen.tbskipwsl          TBSKIPWSL: with paging on, how many
                                     pages still in the translation
                                     buffer the pager passes over when
                                     it chooses a page to take out of a
                                     working set.  0 turns the check
                                     off (and is allowed).  Default 8.

       vax.sysgen.growlim            GROWLIM: with paging on, a working
                                     set at or past its quota takes a
                                     new page without giving one up
                                     only while more than this many
                                     pages are free.  0 is allowed.
                                     Default 63.

       vax.sysgen.mpw_hilimit        MPW_HILIMIT: with paging on, the
                                     modified page writer starts when
                                     more than this many pages are on
                                     the modified page list, and
                                     writes it down to one cluster
                                     (mpw_wrtcluster) below this, as
                                     VMS 7.1's does.  0 is allowed.
                                     Default 500.

       vax.sysgen.mpw_lolimit        MPW_LOLIMIT: the modified page
                                     writer never writes the list
                                     below this many pages, and writes
                                     it down to this when the free
                                     list runs short (freelim).  0 is
                                     allowed.  Default 32.

       vax.sysgen.mpw_wrtcluster     MPW_WRTCLUSTER: the most pages the
                                     modified page writer writes in
                                     one cluster, and so the largest
                                     run of page file blocks it takes
                                     at once.  A multiple of 8 from 16
                                     to 120.  Default 120.

       vax.sysgen.freelim            FREELIM: with paging on, when fewer
                                     than this many pages are on the
                                     free page list, govax reclaims
                                     pages: it writes modified pages it
                                     can, and trims working sets that
                                     grew past their quotas.  0 turns
                                     it off.  Default 32.

       vax.sysgen.freegoal           FREEGOAL: the reclaimer stops when
                                     this many pages are free.  0 is
                                     allowed.  Default 200.

       vax.sysgen.gblsections        GBLSECTIONS: the most global
                                     sections that may exist at once;
                                     $CRMPSC fails with SS$_GSDFULL
                                     past it.  Default 128.

       vax.sysgen.gblpages           GBLPAGES: the global page table
                                     entries the global sections may
                                     use in all, each section its pages
                                     and two more, rounded up to an
                                     even number; $CRMPSC fails with
                                     SS$_GPTFULL past it.  Default 4096.

       vax.sysgen.gblpagfil          GBLPAGFIL: the pages the page-file
                                     global sections may have in all;
                                     $CRMPSC fails with SS$_EXGBLPAGFIL
                                     past it.  Default 4096.

       vax.sysgen.pfcdefault         PFCDEFAULT: with paging on, the
                                     most pages a page fault reads
                                     from a file or the page file at
                                     once, the page faulted and its
                                     neighbors, when the section gave
                                     no cluster size of its own
                                     ($CRMPSC's pfc argument).  0 and
                                     1 read only the page faulted.
                                     From 0 to 127.  Default 32.

       vax.sysgen.virtualpagecnt     VIRTUALPAGECNT: with paging on, how
                                     many pages a new process's program
                                     and control regions (P0 and P1)
                                     may have together: the size of its
                                     page tables, which the two regions
                                     share as they grow.  P1 starts
                                     with 4096 of them (from the user
                                     stack's top up).  $CRETVA and
                                     $EXPREG fail with SS$_VASFULL past
                                     it.  Default INITIALIZE/MEMORY's
                                     /P0 plus /P1
                                     (process 1's).

       vax.sysgen.paging.file        With paging on, the page file: where
                                     pages with no file of their own go
                                     when their memory is needed.  A
                                     file on a mounted volume
                                     (DUA1:[WORK]PAGEFILE.SYS), or a
                                     host file, in quotes
                                     ("./pagefile.sys").  It's
                                     installed as govax starts, after
                                     STARTUP.COM and before your commands
                                     (and again after each
                                     INITIALIZE/MEMORY or
                                     INITIALIZE/VAX): created if it's
                                     missing, and made
                                     vax.sysgen.paging.size blocks if
                                     it isn't, so its space is taken at
                                     once.  Its volume can't be
                                     dismounted while it's open.
                                     Unset, it's the system volume's
                                     [SYSTEM.SYSEXE]PAGEFILE.SYS, or
                                     none when there is no system
                                     volume or it's read-only (another
                                     govax session has it; SHOW MEMORY
                                     says so).  With paging off,
                                     there's no page file.

       vax.sysgen.paging.size        The page file's size in blocks
                                     (pages).  An existing page file of
                                     another size is grown or shrunk to
                                     it (kept at its size, with a
                                     warning, if its volume hasn't room
                                     to grow it).  Default 32768.

       vax.sysgen.paging.defer       true or false: true leaves the page
                                     file unopened until paging first
                                     needs it, instead of installing it
                                     as govax starts.  Default false.

   Debugging switches

       vax.debug.NAME                true or false: the initial state of
                                     the SET DEBUG switch NAME (see HELP
                                     SET DEBUG).  NAME is one of vm, tb,
                                     symbols, exceptions, interrupts,
                                     chm, registers, fulldisasm,
                                     userhalt, keyboard, images,
                                     services, dcl, command, logicals,
                                     devices, process, libinit, rms, or
                                     userstep.
